    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0406093v5</id>
    <title>The Beckman-Quarles theorem for continuous mappings from C^n to C^n</title>
    <updated>2006-09-05T20:02:07Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0406093v5" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0406093v5" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>  Let varphi_n:C^n times C^n-&gt;C, varphi_n((x_1,...,x_n),(y_1,...,y_n))=sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-y_i)^2. We say that f:C^n-&gt;C^n preserves distance d&gt;=0, if for each X,Y in C^n varphi_n(X,Y)=d^2 implies varphi_n(f(X),f(Y))=d^2. We prove: if n&gt;=2 and a continuous f:C^n-&gt;C^n preserves unit distance, then f has a form I circ (rho,...,rho), where I:C^n-&gt;C^n is an affine mapping with orthogonal linear part and rho:C-&gt;C is the identity or the complex conjugation. For n &gt;=3 and bijective f the theorem follows from Theorem 2 in [8].</summary>
