    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0307055v2</id>
    <title>The Beckman-Quarles theorem for mappings from R^2 to F^2, where F is a subfield of a commutative field extending R</title>
    <updated>2005-06-24T20:07:01Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0307055v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0307055v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>  Let F be a subfield of a commutative field extending R. Let φ_2: F^2 \times F^2 \to F, φ_2((x_1,x_2),(y_1,y_2))=(x_1-y_1)^2+(x_2-y_2)^2. We say that f:R^2 \to F^2 preserves distance d \geq 0 if for each x,y \in R^2 |x-y|=d implies φ_2(f(x),f(y))=d^2. We prove that each unit-distance preserving mapping f:R^2 \to F^2 has a form I \circ (ρ,ρ), where ρ: R \to F is a field homomorphism and I: F^2 \to F^2 is an affine mapping with orthogonal linear part.</summary>
